Identity Checks
(PowerShell) Check: VM has no local admin accounts
´´´text
Get-LocalUser | Where-Object { $_.Enabled -eq $true -and $_.Name -ne "Administrator" }
´´´

(PowerShell) Check: VM uses Entra ID login
´´´text
az vm show -g <RG> -n <VM> --query "securityProfile.enableVtpm"
´´´

(PowerShell) Check: Managed Identity is enabled
´´´text
az vm show -g <RG> -n <VM> --query "identity"
´´´
_______________________________________________________________________________
Network Checks

(PowerShell) Check: NIC has NSG
´´´text
$nsg = $nic.NetworkSecurityGroup
if (-not $nsg) { "NIC missing NSG" }
´´´
(Bash) Check: Subnet has NSG
´´´text
az network vnet subnet show -g <RG> --vnet-name <VNET> -n <SUBNET> --query "networkSecurityGroup"
´´´

(Bash) Check: VM has no public IP
´´´text
az network nic show -g <RG> -n <NIC> --query "ipConfigurations[].publicIpAddress"
´´´

(Bash) Check: UDR forces traffic through firewall
´´´text
az network route-table route list -g <RG> --route-table-name <RT>
´´´
_______________________________________________________________________________
Encryption Checks
Check: Disk encryption enabled
´´´text
az disk show -g <RG> -n <DISK> --query "encryption.type"
´´´
_______________________________________________________________________________
Logging Checks
Check: NSG Flow Logs enabled
´´´text
az network watcher flow-log show --resource-group <RG> --name <NW>
´´´

Check: VM Insights enabled
´´´text
Get-AzVMExtension -ResourceGroupName <RG> -VMName <VM> | Where-Object { $_.Name -like "*AzureMonitor*" }
´´´
_______________________________________________________________________________
Noise Reduction Techniques

Only flag violations, not compliant resources
Instead of dumping all VM data, only output findings:
PowerShell
´´´text
if ($vmHasIssue) {
    [PSCustomObject]@{
        VMName = $vm.Name
        Issue  = $issueDescription
    }
}
´´´

Suppress “expected” warnings
If the environment intentionally allows:
* Public IPs for jumpboxes
* Specific extensions
* Specific firewall bypasses
Add an allow‑list:
PowerShell
´´´text
$allowedVMs = @("jumpbox01", "bastion01")
if ($allowedVMs -contains $vm.Name) { continue }
´´´

Add severity levels
PowerShell
´´´text
[PSCustomObject]@{
    VMName   = $vm.Name
    Severity = "High"
    Issue    = "Missing NSG"
}
´´´

Add context to reduce false positives
Example: NSG missing but subnet NSG exists → not a violation.
PowerShell
´´´text
if (-not $nicNSG -and $subnetNSG) {
    # Not a violation
    continue
}
´´´
_______________________________________________________________________________
Improved Terraform/Bicep Patterns
Use parameterized policy assignments
bicep
´´´text
param policyName string
param scope string

resource assign 'Microsoft.Authorization/policyAssignments@2023-03-01' = {
  name: policyName
  properties: {
    scope: scope
    policyDefinitionId: policyDefinition.id
  }
}
´´´

Use Terraform modules
hcl
´´´text
module "vm_governance" {
  source = "./modules/vm-governance"
  scope  = var.scope
}
´´´
